6. Population Sources

The architecture should not depend upon unauthorized access to private AI conversations.

Possible population sources include:

6.1 Explicitly opted-in users

Participants knowingly contribute selected information for population modeling.

6.2 Dedicated research panels

People join GPT Polls specifically to contribute preference and opinion information.

6.3 Public survey datasets

Existing polling, census, academic, market, and behavioral datasets can provide priors and calibration.

6.4 Organization-owned datasets

A company could construct a private GPT Polls population from customers who have consented to appropriate research uses.

6.5 User-created populations

A researcher might manually define:

* 500 hypothetical buyers,
* 1,000 fictional voters,
* six customer personas,
* characters inside a story world,
* employees across organizational roles.

6.6 Hybrid populations

Real aggregate distributions can be combined with synthetic augmentation.

The downstream engine remains largely unchanged regardless of population source.

This makes GPT Polls fundamentally a population simulation architecture, not merely a feature dependent upon one platform’s user data.

