13. Mirror Polls

One of the strongest applications is measuring the difference between what people prefer and what they believe others prefer.

Ask each modeled respondent:

Question A

What do you personally want?

Question B

What do you think most other people want?

Then:

MirrorGap =
P(personal\ preference)
-
P(perceived\ consensus)

Example:

Personally prefers minimalist gray interiors:
31%
Believes most people prefer minimalist gray interiors:
67%

A large gap may suggest a modeled form of:

* pluralistic ignorance,
* social projection,
* information cascade,
* false consensus,
* mistaken norm perception.

The system can extend recursively.

Ask:

What do you think companies believe consumers want?

Then compare:

What I want
        ↓
What I think others want
        ↓
What I think companies think others want

This produces a recursive social-model stack.

