14. Preference Discovery Versus Preference Shaping

A major design requirement is separating:

Measurement

from:

Intervention

A system designed to measure preferences should not quietly incorporate the outputs of advertising campaigns, sponsors, or previous GPT Polls predictions in a way that converts prediction into self-fulfilling consensus.

Therefore:

Measure preference separately from attempts to shape preference.

And:

Never convert perceived consensus into observed consensus.

This matters because populations can respond to their beliefs about populations.

Showing someone:

“82% of people prefer X”

can itself change subsequent preference.

GPT Polls therefore needs explicit separation between:

Unexposed population
Population exposed to social signal X
Population exposed to message Y
Observed post-exposure response

That turns persuasion into an observable experimental variable instead of invisible contamination.

