47. Failure Modes

GPT Polls could fail in several predictable ways.

47.1 Synthetic monoculture

If all respondents are generated by the same underlying model, apparent diversity may be superficial.

Mitigation:

* multiple models,
* heterogeneous prompts,
* empirical calibration,
* external datasets,
* adversarial respondent generation.

⸻

47.2 Demographic caricature

Respondents could become stereotypes.

Mitigation:

* individual-level latent variation,
* weak demographic priors,
* real calibration,
* intersectional uncertainty,
* avoidance of deterministic demographic assumptions.

⸻

47.3 Model laundering

A single model opinion could masquerade as population consensus simply because it was sampled repeatedly.

Mitigation:

* diversity diagnostics,
* effective population dimensionality,
* cross-model comparison,
* provenance disclosure.

⸻

47.4 False precision

A result such as:

63.14%

may imply certainty the system does not possess.

Mitigation:

* credible intervals,
* stability scores,
* rounded values,
* explicit uncertainty.

⸻

47.5 Recursive collapse

The system trains increasingly on its own predictions.

Mitigation:

* immutable prediction history,
* external calibration,
* source separation,
* Anchor Spine.

⸻

47.6 Self-fulfilling polls

Publishing a prediction changes the population being predicted.

Mitigation:

* distinguish pre-exposure and post-exposure populations,
* record poll exposure,
* model information contamination.

⸻

47.7 Privacy leakage

Rare combinations of attributes could identify individuals.

Mitigation:

* cohort minimums,
* aggregation,
* feature suppression,
* privacy budgets,
* de-identification.

⸻

47.8 Manipulative optimization

The system could be used to search aggressively for psychological vulnerabilities.

Mitigation:

* population-level rather than person-specific optimization,
* restrictions around sensitive vulnerabilities,
* transparency,
* explicit distinction between research and exploitation.

⸻

